    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d922">

                <pb facs="1216" n="1206" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg1206"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5034/1–1245: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-12T16:00:00+07:00">January 12, 1945—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received January 12—11:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>50. Embassy believes article on registration of auto companies in China December
                    9 issue <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Commerce Weekly</hi> substantially
                    correct, but over-simplified. Suggest, with Helmick’s<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d922-f1"/> concurrence, that Department advise Commerce to be
                    cautious in advising American companies to register pending hoped-for
                    clarification Chinese company law on subject of qualification and admission of
                    foreign corporations. Will report further.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d922-f1" n="2" xml:id="d922-f1-content">
                <p>Judge Milton J. Helmick, sent to China in November 1944 by the Department of
                    State to survey the effects of Chinese laws, regulations, and courts on American
                    firms having commercial interests in that country.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
